10:06 < johnny9dev> I am personally very curious about when building a harness was very valuable. My experience has been each time I build one, the new model just does all of what i needed already so I just wasted a lot of time when I could have been focused on my work and waiting for the new model 
10:06 -!- jerryf [~jerryf@user/jerryf] has joined #bitcoin-core-dev
10:06 -!- jonatack [~jonatack@user/jonatack] has quit [Ping timeout: 246 seconds]
10:07 -!- jonatack [~jonatack@user/jonatack] has joined #bitcoin-core-dev
10:07 < johnny9dev> or an example of a good working review harness would be interesting
10:09 < Rob1Ham> johnny9dev: I've found the harness to be a very signifigant differentiator in the quality of the outputs, in being able to direct certain patterns/behaviors in finding issues
10:09 -!- dzxzg [~instrumen@user/dzxzg] has quit [Quit: onversation terminated!]
10:10 < Rob1Ham> I've also been able to run circles out of claude/chatgpt with my harness using kimi k3/deepseekv4pro glm 5.3 etc
10:11 < Rob1Ham> A harness also has dials that allow you to deal with precision and recall of scans, meaning do you want to only surface 100% true confirmed positive issues, or just try to get all things that are plausibly wrong with a code base, so you can use that dialing and fine tuning for different parts of the software development lifecycle based on the auidence consuming it and the intent of the
10:11 < Rob1Ham> review.
10:11 -!- jadi [~jadi@s216-232-13-176.bc.hsia.telus.net] has joined #bitcoin-core-dev
10:12 -!- jerryf [~jerryf@user/jerryf] has quit [Remote host closed the connection]
10:12 -!- jerryf [~jerryf@user/jerryf] has joined #bitcoin-core-dev
10:13 -!- Guest18 [~Guest79@d75-157-212-231.bchsia.telus.net] has joined #bitcoin-core-dev
10:13 < johnny9dev> Are you able to share your harness publically?
10:13 < Rob1Ham> johnny9dev:  My goal is to do so! I'm trying to clean up the user experience and make it easy to configure all of the kinds of scans.
10:14 < Rob1Ham> My inital harness was built in 30 minutes using kimi k3 in opencode to set up several loops of issues
10:14 -!- jerryf [~jerryf@user/jerryf] has quit [Remote host closed the connection]
10:14 < Rob1Ham> which was a step up in performance when compared to just using a single session window
10:14 -!- jerryf [~jerryf@user/jerryf] has joined #bitcoin-core-dev
10:14 < Rob1Ham> The intention is to have it tested among codebases i've scanned previoulsy, and use that for feedback and giving maintainers a time to review stuff before making it generally available, and its configurable so you can switch out models however you want.
10:15 < Rob1Ham> and open source so other projects or teams can modify it to meet their own needs, or just take the concepts and have people reimpliment it however they wish
10:15 < johnny9dev> Meaning instead of doing a full scan you have different prompts run separately for targeted classes of issues?
10:16 -!- jerryf [~jerryf@user/jerryf] has quit [Remote host closed the connection]
10:16 -!- jerryf [~jerryf@user/jerryf] has joined #bitcoin-core-dev
10:18 -!- jerryf [~jerryf@user/jerryf] has quit [Remote host closed the connection]
10:18 < Rob1Ham> johnny9dev: exactly, I have a general quick scan looking at targetted issues such as entropy, key misamangemnet, supplychain issues, possible loss of fund codepaths etc, versus ones that are more open ended to let it be creative
10:18 < Rob1Ham> It also somewhat turns into a bespoke harness for each codebase as it does more scans over time to identify issues
10:18 -!- jerryf [~jerryf@user/jerryf] has joined #bitcoin-core-dev
10:19 < Rob1Ham> I've also done stuff like built a library hook, where it will scan a codebase, see which cryptographic concepts are being utilized, and then look for divergences between the academic theory versus code implmentations to find issues
10:20 < johnny9dev> it certainly sounds like that will be valuable as a shared harness project  
10:21 < Rob1Ham> I'm far from the final expert, so if nothing else can be a handy resource others can use to build even better security tools
